<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Sauromatae</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iranian-speaking nomads between the Don and the southern Urals, ancestors of the Sarmatians, remembered by Herodotus for their armed and riding women.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> places the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sarmatians">Sauromatae</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Don</a>, beyond the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/scythia">Royal Scythians</a>, in the 5th century BCE, and tells the legend that they descended from Scythian men and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazons">Amazons</a>. Archaeologists link them to the Early Sarmatian or Sauromatian culture of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Urals</a>, which took shape around the 6th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived as pastoral nomads moving between seasonal pastures, burying their dead in kurgans with weapons, bronze mirrors and stone altars. A striking feature of their graves is the number of women buried with arrows, swords and horse gear, which matches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>' remark that their women rode, hunted and fought, and could not marry before killing an enemy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 4th century BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sarmatians">Sauromatae</a> merged into the larger Sarmatian confederations, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aorsi">Aorsi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraces">Siraces</a>, which pushed west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Don</a> and displaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. Their name largely gives way to 'Sarmatians' in later Greek and Roman sources.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="nomadic confederation, 600 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">nomadic confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:47.76%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iranian steppe religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Scythian (Eastern Iranian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sauromatians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=sauromatae" aria-label="Sauromatae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5645 235 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sauromatae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5965 491h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5965 491h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=sauromatae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
